WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.437 --> 00:00:02.994
- There are people with incredible talent

00:00:02.994 --> 00:00:05.713
and no motivation and then
there are other people

00:00:05.713 --> 00:00:07.449
that are willing to work their ass off.

00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:09.118
It's above winning a contest.

00:00:09.118 --> 00:00:13.854
It's about that one time
just, yeah I did that!

00:00:13.854 --> 00:00:16.501
This is what I love
doing more than anything

00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:18.785
and I would do it for free.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.283
Any day of the week.

00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:23.386
There were times when I
started our company, Birdhouse,

00:00:23.386 --> 00:00:26.694
in the first three years in,
we were ready to give in.

00:00:26.694 --> 00:00:29.294
He's an American professional
skateboarder, actor,

00:00:29.294 --> 00:00:32.407
and owner of the skateboard
company, Birdhouse.

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:35.373
He's widely considered
to be one of the pioneers

00:00:35.373 --> 00:00:36.700
and most successful influencers

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.150
of modern vertical skateboarding.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.073
Throughout his career,

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.669
he's made a number of appearances
in films, other media,

00:00:42.669 --> 00:00:43.502
and his own line of video games.

00:00:43.502 --> 00:00:46.666
He's Tony Hawk and here's my take

00:00:46.666 --> 00:00:48.867
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:50.528
Rule number three is my personal favorite

00:00:50.528 --> 00:00:52.461
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:52.461 --> 00:00:53.604
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:53.604 --> 00:00:54.914
Also, as Tony's talking,

00:00:54.914 --> 00:00:56.514
if he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:56.514 --> 00:00:58.014
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:58.014 --> 00:00:59.893
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.357
can be inspired as well.

00:01:01.357 --> 00:01:02.190
Enjoy!

00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:08.046
(upbeat music)

00:01:15.647 --> 00:01:17.825
- It wasn't until I started
my own team when I realized

00:01:17.825 --> 00:01:21.492
that there are people
with incredible talent

00:01:22.945 --> 00:01:25.794
and no motivation and then
there are other people

00:01:25.794 --> 00:01:28.735
that are willing to work
their ass off who don't have

00:01:28.735 --> 00:01:32.177
a natural ability but
will become successful

00:01:32.177 --> 00:01:34.343
because they're so determined

00:01:34.343 --> 00:01:38.458
and I saw it especially
in a couple of guys

00:01:38.458 --> 00:01:40.740
that I first put on the Birdhouse team,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.054
namely Andrew Reynolds, Matt Beach.

00:01:44.054 --> 00:01:46.304
Matt Beach was, in my eyes,

00:01:47.445 --> 00:01:49.960
the most naturally talented skateboarder

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.131
that I've ever seen.

00:01:51.131 --> 00:01:53.714
Everything came easy to him and, you know,

00:01:53.714 --> 00:01:55.630
when I would request
these guys to go on tour,

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:57.425
all I ask of them is that they skate

00:01:57.425 --> 00:01:59.294
for exhibitions and whatnot.

00:01:59.294 --> 00:02:00.752
Matt wouldn't skate half the time.

00:02:00.752 --> 00:02:02.150
I flew him to Europe.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.347
I didn't have any money
then in our company

00:02:04.347 --> 00:02:06.276
but I was flying these guys
to Europe for competitions.

00:02:06.276 --> 00:02:08.281
He would go to the contests
and not feel like skating

00:02:08.281 --> 00:02:10.419
and I was like, how can you do that?

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.018
You know, I was thinking, not
just to me but to yourself.

00:02:13.018 --> 00:02:17.103
You have this incredible
talent and you just don't care

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:20.238
and then I saw Andrew
Reynolds who was this scrappy

00:02:20.238 --> 00:02:24.388
little guy taking not very
clean in terms of his style

00:02:24.388 --> 00:02:26.464
but trying some of the hardest stuff,

00:02:26.464 --> 00:02:29.753
taking the heaviest hits but
always skating his ass off

00:02:29.753 --> 00:02:32.088
and always trying and
he has become probably

00:02:32.088 --> 00:02:35.588
the most well respected
street skater today.

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:37.380
Andrew Reynolds, I mean
he formed Baker Brand,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.807
Deathwish Skateboards.

00:02:38.807 --> 00:02:43.108
The community of the most
hard core skater these days

00:02:43.108 --> 00:02:46.846
call him The Boss because
he developed his style

00:02:46.846 --> 00:02:50.186
because he worked his ass off
and he's the shining example

00:02:50.186 --> 00:02:51.269
of hard work.

00:02:52.746 --> 00:02:54.966
- [Announcer] Tony Hawk is
the number one recognizable

00:02:54.966 --> 00:02:59.825
sports figure over Shaq,
over Iverson, over MJ even.

00:02:59.825 --> 00:03:01.829
- I just focus on something
and I have to do it.

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:05.128
I'll either get hurt, taken
to the hospital trying it,

00:03:05.128 --> 00:03:08.465
or I'm going to make it and
that was the same for doing

00:03:08.465 --> 00:03:10.500
the 900 at the X Games.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.517
It was my time to finally
do it or to be carted off

00:03:13.517 --> 00:03:15.633
and tried another day.

00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:18.027
- [Announcer] Last try
right here, Tony Hawk, 900.

00:03:18.027 --> 00:03:19.756
- At some point, you
just tune everything out

00:03:19.756 --> 00:03:23.089
and you say this is it, you got to do it.

00:03:29.498 --> 00:03:33.075
- [Announcer] Literally,
Pier 30 shook and we felt

00:03:33.075 --> 00:03:35.040
the ground swell.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.100
I wasn't sure if it was an
earthquake or somebody out there

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.124
doing something special.

00:03:39.124 --> 00:03:40.818
That moment was probably burned

00:03:40.818 --> 00:03:42.775
in most of our minds forever.

00:03:42.775 --> 00:03:44.568
- I knew I wanted to be
in the skating industry.

00:03:44.568 --> 00:03:45.620
I loved skating too much.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.868
I wanted to be in it.

00:03:46.868 --> 00:03:48.184
I didn't know if I would make
a living as a professional

00:03:48.184 --> 00:03:51.797
but I was going to be, I was
devoted to skateboarding.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:55.714
So I refinanced my house
and took all the money

00:03:56.957 --> 00:03:59.245
and started a skateboard
company which seems like

00:03:59.245 --> 00:04:01.195
the stupidest thing in the
world to do when skating's dying

00:04:01.195 --> 00:04:04.445
but it was exactly what I wanted to do.

00:04:05.375 --> 00:04:07.707
I wanted to be a company owner
and be behind the scenes.

00:04:07.707 --> 00:04:11.596
I quit my sponsor and started
and for about three years,

00:04:11.596 --> 00:04:15.177
struggled very hard with my partner and I,

00:04:15.177 --> 00:04:17.203
we had pooled our money together.

00:04:17.203 --> 00:04:21.828
He was an ex-pro skater as
well and we just did Birdhouse

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:26.093
and did whatever we could
to get by and all the team

00:04:26.093 --> 00:04:27.855
was staying on my couch.

00:04:27.855 --> 00:04:29.463
I literally, while I'm changing diapers,

00:04:29.463 --> 00:04:32.020
I'm like driving guys to these
spots that they're going to

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.048
get arrested at because
there's no skate parks

00:04:35.048 --> 00:04:37.538
so they're going to go ride schoolyards

00:04:37.538 --> 00:04:40.481
and it was such a strange existence

00:04:40.481 --> 00:04:43.146
but I loved it because
it was skateboarding

00:04:43.146 --> 00:04:45.729
and it was exactly what I wanted to do

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:48.498
and so we really hunkered down.

00:04:48.498 --> 00:04:52.406
I was living on Taco Bell
and peanut butter and jelly

00:04:52.406 --> 00:04:53.788
and Top Ramen.

00:04:53.788 --> 00:04:55.410
- Yum.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.347
- Probably three years, for sure,

00:04:57.347 --> 00:04:59.016
but I didn't care 'cause it was like,

00:04:59.016 --> 00:05:00.325
I still got to do what I love.

00:05:00.325 --> 00:05:01.585
That's the thing is that people think,

00:05:01.585 --> 00:05:02.593
was it a struggle?

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:04.851
And it's like well, no,
because I loved it too much.

00:05:04.851 --> 00:05:08.261
I still got to go skate
and I had time to skate.

00:05:08.261 --> 00:05:11.171
I didn't have to go sit behind
a computer for eight hours

00:05:11.171 --> 00:05:13.293
and then hope I get time to skate.

00:05:13.293 --> 00:05:15.960
- [Male] Okay, ready and action.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.894
- [Male 2] Three, two, one.

00:05:19.894 --> 00:05:20.727
Go!

00:05:22.097 --> 00:05:24.514
(rock music)

00:05:28.297 --> 00:05:31.047
(crowd cheering)

00:05:33.494 --> 00:05:34.521
- Alright, guys.

00:05:34.521 --> 00:05:35.761
- I'm glad you're alright.

00:05:35.761 --> 00:05:36.594
- Let's not do that again.

00:05:36.594 --> 00:05:37.427
(group laughing)

00:05:37.427 --> 00:05:38.844
- [Male] I agree.

00:05:40.168 --> 00:05:42.577
(rock music)

00:05:42.577 --> 00:05:44.135
- [Group] Oh!

00:05:44.135 --> 00:05:45.783
- That's great.

00:05:45.783 --> 00:05:48.200
(rock music)

00:05:52.668 --> 00:05:54.373
- I know there are so many
kids out there that feel

00:05:54.373 --> 00:05:56.373
the way that I did when I was a kid,

00:05:56.373 --> 00:06:00.975
just awkward and not excelling
at one thing in particular

00:06:00.975 --> 00:06:05.142
and not cool and skateboarding
was my way out of that

00:06:07.078 --> 00:06:10.097
and I hope that, if
not with skateboarding,

00:06:10.097 --> 00:06:11.965
they find it with something else they do

00:06:11.965 --> 00:06:13.705
but I hope that they're not afraid

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:15.201
to try something
different 'cause you can't

00:06:15.201 --> 00:06:16.270
listen to the haters.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:17.464
That's my best advice to anyone.

00:06:17.464 --> 00:06:19.896
If I had listened to the haters,

00:06:19.896 --> 00:06:21.803
I would have quit a long time ago.

00:06:21.803 --> 00:06:23.579
I would have quit when I was 11 years old.

00:06:23.579 --> 00:06:26.582
I used to get bashed
by professional skaters

00:06:26.582 --> 00:06:31.159
because my style was
dorky or it was all circus

00:06:31.159 --> 00:06:32.326
but you can't.

00:06:34.609 --> 00:06:37.442
You've got to believe in yourself.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:40.543
As far as I know, no one's
done anything like this,

00:06:40.543 --> 00:06:42.616
and so people keep
asking me questions like,

00:06:42.616 --> 00:06:44.173
well, how do you approach it?

00:06:44.173 --> 00:06:46.040
I don't know.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.873
I'm just going to find
out the hard way, I guess.

00:06:50.871 --> 00:06:52.421
It feels like the first
time I tried the loop.

00:06:52.421 --> 00:06:54.792
I just walked up to it and was like, holy,

00:06:54.792 --> 00:06:56.209
what have I done?

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.834
But I'm up for the challenge.

00:06:59.834 --> 00:07:02.417
(upbeat music)

00:07:09.961 --> 00:07:10.794
Whoo.

00:07:16.572 --> 00:07:17.489
Second try.

00:07:21.506 --> 00:07:23.339
I can't throw that up.

00:07:24.367 --> 00:07:28.034
("Somebody Grab the Wheel")

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:53.380
(crowd cheering)

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:54.589
- Yeah, I think Tony's going to do it.

00:07:54.589 --> 00:07:58.422
I think it's just going
to be hanging that angle.

00:08:01.934 --> 00:08:03.855
- That was like a half head,
half knee slide but hey,

00:08:03.855 --> 00:08:06.438
I didn't hit my hip so success.

00:08:11.782 --> 00:08:14.532
Okay, get these pads out of here.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.452
- It's coming down.

00:08:17.452 --> 00:08:18.452
He's got it.

00:08:21.256 --> 00:08:24.923
("Somebody Grab the Wheel")

00:08:31.708 --> 00:08:32.791
- Here we go.

00:08:45.521 --> 00:08:48.271
(crowd cheering)

00:08:57.772 --> 00:08:59.800
- I'm just so excited.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.967
I can't believe it worked.

00:09:07.001 --> 00:09:08.808
- [Pharrell] Does running
the Tony Hawk empire

00:09:08.808 --> 00:09:12.480
give you the same
satisfaction as the nine golds

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:14.432
at the X Games?

00:09:14.432 --> 00:09:17.982
- I love the challenge of
the business aspects of it

00:09:17.982 --> 00:09:20.554
but I love that it's still fun.

00:09:20.554 --> 00:09:21.451
- Yeah.

00:09:21.451 --> 00:09:23.167
- I mean, the stuff that I
choose to do is only stuff

00:09:23.167 --> 00:09:24.787
that I really am
passionate about and stuff

00:09:24.787 --> 00:09:26.169
that I will enjoy seeing through.

00:09:26.169 --> 00:09:27.969
To be honest,

00:09:27.969 --> 00:09:31.969
the high that I go for
that is always driving me

00:09:34.001 --> 00:09:36.418
is when I land something new,

00:09:37.665 --> 00:09:39.608
when I'm trying to learn a new trick

00:09:39.608 --> 00:09:41.145
and I make it for the first time,

00:09:41.145 --> 00:09:42.975
even if it's something
someone else has already done,

00:09:42.975 --> 00:09:47.142
for me, that push and that
adrenaline and the confidence

00:09:51.421 --> 00:09:54.837
of thinking I can do this
and then finally doing it,

00:09:54.837 --> 00:09:56.800
you know, seeing it
through and when I land it,

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:00.083
that's the buzz, that's the
high for me above all else,

00:10:00.083 --> 00:10:02.193
above winning a contest.

00:10:02.193 --> 00:10:06.548
It's about that one time
just, yeah I did that!

00:10:06.548 --> 00:10:07.452
- Yeah.

00:10:07.452 --> 00:10:09.200
- And I'm older now and now
when I get that feeling,

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.367
it's further between when
I feel it and so when I do,

00:10:14.926 --> 00:10:16.203
it's way more intense.

00:10:16.203 --> 00:10:17.589
- It's like landing your first kick flip.

00:10:17.589 --> 00:10:19.060
- It's, yeah, that's what it feels like.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:20.498
Every time.

00:10:20.498 --> 00:10:21.667
It doesn't look as intimidating
when it's not sitting

00:10:21.667 --> 00:10:23.537
on top of a building structure.

00:10:23.537 --> 00:10:28.154
The jump is always scary
'cause you want all your skin

00:10:28.154 --> 00:10:30.785
anywhere on your legs covered.

00:10:30.785 --> 00:10:32.215
- [Cameraman] Are you sure
it's just not for fashion?

00:10:32.215 --> 00:10:33.358
- Well, that too.

00:10:33.358 --> 00:10:35.513
This is my mega board.

00:10:35.513 --> 00:10:36.994
- [Cameraman] Mega.

00:10:36.994 --> 00:10:39.577
(wind blowing)

00:11:05.409 --> 00:11:06.242
Whoo!

00:11:09.051 --> 00:11:10.081
Yeah!

00:11:10.081 --> 00:11:11.279
- I did it!

00:11:11.279 --> 00:11:12.180
(cameraman laughs)

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:13.616
I wanted to figure out
how I could remove myself

00:11:13.616 --> 00:11:15.377
from competition and still be a pro skater

00:11:15.377 --> 00:11:18.282
and that had not been done yet

00:11:18.282 --> 00:11:21.617
because if you're not going to compete,

00:11:21.617 --> 00:11:24.552
the magazines aren't going to cover you,

00:11:24.552 --> 00:11:27.770
your sponsors are likely
to drop you and the kids

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:31.106
who are buying your products
are going to forget about you

00:11:31.106 --> 00:11:35.635
and there was no such thing
as a video skater back then.

00:11:35.635 --> 00:11:37.909
That hadn't really come into play yet.

00:11:37.909 --> 00:11:39.789
- Mm Hmm (affirmative)

00:11:39.789 --> 00:11:41.706
- There was no YouTube.

00:11:41.706 --> 00:11:43.885
The skate videos were usually
based on the best guys

00:11:43.885 --> 00:11:48.296
in the competitions and so
I went and talked to Stacy.

00:11:48.296 --> 00:11:50.776
- Who was your boss at the time?

00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:53.482
- Stacy Peralta was my coach
and I actually had my brother

00:11:53.482 --> 00:11:57.400
come with me as back up and
he approached Stacy and said,

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:02.082
"Look, Tony's really having a
problem with the competitions.

00:12:02.082 --> 00:12:04.049
"It's kind of wrecking him.

00:12:04.049 --> 00:12:05.804
"It's ruining the fun of skating."

00:12:05.804 --> 00:12:08.521
And I told him that and so he said,

00:12:08.521 --> 00:12:10.650
"Well, I understand.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:13.149
"Maybe you might want to take a break

00:12:13.149 --> 00:12:14.521
"but don't give it up completely

00:12:14.521 --> 00:12:17.510
"'cause you may want to come back.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:20.706
"You may enjoy it and I
don't know if we're going to

00:12:20.706 --> 00:12:23.421
"be able to sustain your career
if you're not competing."

00:12:23.421 --> 00:12:25.008
And I did take a break.

00:12:25.008 --> 00:12:27.425
I stopped competing for
awhile, almost a year,

00:12:27.425 --> 00:12:31.171
maybe a little less and
when I came back to it,

00:12:31.171 --> 00:12:33.657
I came back with sort
of a fresh perspective

00:12:33.657 --> 00:12:36.500
that I don't really care how it lays out.

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:39.448
I'm going to go and do my best
and I'm going to take chances

00:12:39.448 --> 00:12:42.581
that I maybe hadn't taken
before and if that puts me

00:12:42.581 --> 00:12:46.331
in last place then so
be it but if I succeed,

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:50.316
I'm going to be on this
whole other level of skating.

00:12:50.316 --> 00:12:51.217
- Mm Hmm (affirmative)

00:12:51.217 --> 00:12:53.506
- And that's what happened and
the contests that I won then,

00:12:53.506 --> 00:12:58.088
I won by a long shot
'cause I was doing stuff

00:12:58.088 --> 00:13:00.133
that was experimental and stuff

00:13:00.133 --> 00:13:03.103
that I wasn't really confident
with but I could pull it

00:13:03.103 --> 00:13:05.774
through somehow and if it didn't happen

00:13:05.774 --> 00:13:08.704
then I didn't do well at
all and I stopped caring

00:13:08.704 --> 00:13:11.121
about the end result so much.

00:13:22.129 --> 00:13:26.296
("Police Truck" by Dead Kennedys)

00:14:32.552 --> 00:14:35.811
- What is it that makes
you real special --

00:14:35.811 --> 00:14:37.577
- Well, for one, I never quit.

00:14:37.577 --> 00:14:38.934
I never stopped skating.

00:14:38.934 --> 00:14:42.791
So, I never took a hiatus, say
five years and came in like,

00:14:42.791 --> 00:14:45.132
oh, skating's hot now I'm
going to get back into it.

00:14:45.132 --> 00:14:49.299
So I had that consistency
but I think that, for me,

00:14:51.488 --> 00:14:54.672
I really liked learning new
techniques and a lot of people,

00:14:54.672 --> 00:14:56.337
they did their skating sticking

00:14:56.337 --> 00:14:58.319
with this one style of skating,

00:14:58.319 --> 00:15:02.944
this one set of tricks and
don't want to branch out

00:15:02.944 --> 00:15:04.821
because they're afraid
they won't look cool

00:15:04.821 --> 00:15:06.253
doing it or whatever.

00:15:06.253 --> 00:15:08.300
I always wanted to try
the different techniques

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:09.685
even if I stumbled on 'em.

00:15:09.685 --> 00:15:13.484
I knew that in the end it would
help me be more well rounded

00:15:13.484 --> 00:15:15.770
and I think that's probably
what it is that I just wanted

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:17.687
to try to learn it all.

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.308
- [Announcer] Going for something big.

00:15:20.308 --> 00:15:21.379
He's going for a flip.

00:15:21.379 --> 00:15:23.447
He's going for something new.

00:15:23.447 --> 00:15:27.280
Crowd seems to be
anticipating something good.

00:15:28.249 --> 00:15:31.709
(crowd cheering)

00:15:31.709 --> 00:15:33.745
We've seen that he can
fly high and here it goes.

00:15:33.745 --> 00:15:35.542
Here's Tony!

00:15:35.542 --> 00:15:37.042
He's in the water!

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:39.883
Takes a swim.

00:15:40.941 --> 00:15:42.939
(crowd cheering)

00:15:42.939 --> 00:15:44.059
And the crowd loved that.

00:15:44.059 --> 00:15:45.600
That skateboard's gone.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:46.773
Actually, they'll be a few
people diving in there,

00:15:46.773 --> 00:15:48.856
trying to fetch that one.

00:15:49.903 --> 00:15:51.507
Tony Hawk.

00:15:51.507 --> 00:15:52.581
- What the heck kind of move was that?

00:15:52.581 --> 00:15:56.542
- I don't know, it's the
big gap to the snake canyon.

00:15:56.542 --> 00:16:00.459
- A brand new move by
Tony Hawk into the water.

00:16:01.683 --> 00:16:02.689
- [Announcer] Tony Hawk,

00:16:02.689 --> 00:16:04.542
always a crowd pleaser
and no exception there

00:16:04.542 --> 00:16:08.125
and Tony Hawk set off
at a blistering pace.

00:16:09.957 --> 00:16:14.124
He had to clear the dock
and cleared it by a mile.

00:16:15.547 --> 00:16:17.691
What a performer.

00:16:17.691 --> 00:16:21.249
(crowd cheering)

00:16:21.249 --> 00:16:23.501
See it from behind again.

00:16:23.501 --> 00:16:27.172
Scooting his way across
the arena, off that ramp,

00:16:27.172 --> 00:16:29.089
hit it at the diagonal.

00:16:31.006 --> 00:16:32.339
Wonderful stuff.

00:16:33.648 --> 00:16:34.981
What an end.

00:16:34.981 --> 00:16:36.814
- If you're convinced
that you're going to fail

00:16:36.814 --> 00:16:38.308
or fall or get hurt,

00:16:38.308 --> 00:16:41.675
that will come to fruition
because that's what

00:16:41.675 --> 00:16:42.836
you're visualizing.

00:16:42.836 --> 00:16:46.159
I never, I was willing
to get hurt along the way

00:16:46.159 --> 00:16:48.869
but I didn't visualize myself
getting hurt along the way

00:16:48.869 --> 00:16:52.877
and I think that's what
stops a lot of people

00:16:52.877 --> 00:16:54.864
and that's really what
separates a lot of people

00:16:54.864 --> 00:16:59.518
from being world champions or
from being as good as they can

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:01.962
because they stop at some point saying,

00:17:01.962 --> 00:17:05.016
"Oh no, I'm not capable of this,"

00:17:05.016 --> 00:17:07.265
or "I'll probably get hurt doing this,"

00:17:07.265 --> 00:17:09.011
and if you approach anything
with that hesitation,

00:17:09.011 --> 00:17:10.540
you're going to fail.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:13.998
I always approached it
like, this is going to work.

00:17:13.998 --> 00:17:16.491
I'm going to figure this
out even if it takes me

00:17:16.491 --> 00:17:18.801
hundreds of attempts

00:17:18.801 --> 00:17:22.968
and I wasn't, the idea of falling
and getting back up again,

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:27.319
that didn't really bother me at all.

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:29.736
(rock music)

00:18:27.444 --> 00:18:28.759
I don't really need the money.

00:18:28.759 --> 00:18:30.746
I don't need the money anymore.

00:18:30.746 --> 00:18:34.746
I made dream money from
video games, like stuff,

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:37.769
things that I would never imagine.

00:18:37.769 --> 00:18:41.219
When people say, "Is
this what you dreamt?"

00:18:41.219 --> 00:18:42.809
I didn't dream any of this.

00:18:42.809 --> 00:18:46.188
None of this was possible
or even considered a reality

00:18:46.188 --> 00:18:48.008
when I was a kid.

00:18:48.008 --> 00:18:49.066
When I started skating,

00:18:49.066 --> 00:18:52.181
the best you could hope for
was your picture in a magazine,

00:18:52.181 --> 00:18:55.514
maybe a pro board, maybe, and free gear.

00:18:57.672 --> 00:18:59.550
That's it and then once you
reach an age of responsibility

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:02.014
and this is I'm talking
about when I was like 12,

00:19:02.014 --> 00:19:04.355
once you reach an age of
responsibility say 18,

00:19:04.355 --> 00:19:07.557
your career is over 'cause
you can't make money doing it.

00:19:07.557 --> 00:19:09.148
You can't do it into your adult life.

00:19:09.148 --> 00:19:12.510
Obviously, I'm hugely
thankful to still be able

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:13.512
to do this for a living.

00:19:13.512 --> 00:19:16.239
This is what I love
doing more than anything

00:19:16.239 --> 00:19:20.263
and I would do it for
free any day of the week,

00:19:20.263 --> 00:19:22.108
at any given time.

00:19:22.108 --> 00:19:24.572
I'd happily just go skate.

00:19:24.572 --> 00:19:27.635
It just happens to be the
thing that I'm successful at

00:19:27.635 --> 00:19:30.469
so I get to do what I love for a living

00:19:30.469 --> 00:19:31.875
and that's living the dream.

00:19:31.875 --> 00:19:33.314
I mean, living the dream,

00:19:33.314 --> 00:19:34.957
I'm not talking about financial success.

00:19:34.957 --> 00:19:36.950
I'm talking about loving what you do.

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:40.744
If you love art and you
do art and maybe you get

00:19:40.744 --> 00:19:42.910
a little bit of success at it,

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:44.910
you're living the dream.

00:19:46.179 --> 00:19:48.596
(punk music)

00:20:35.681 --> 00:20:37.969
There were times when I
started our company, Birdhouse,

00:20:37.969 --> 00:20:41.156
in the first three years in,
we were ready to give in.

00:20:41.156 --> 00:20:44.142
The sales just weren't there.

00:20:44.142 --> 00:20:48.401
The interest wasn't there
and same with my skating.

00:20:48.401 --> 00:20:52.898
I was still improving my
skills but for no audience.

00:20:52.898 --> 00:20:56.324
So it was definitely
difficult but we embraced

00:20:56.324 --> 00:20:58.950
those challenges too and
especially as athletes,

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:03.117
someone poses a challenge to
you and you have to figure out

00:21:04.606 --> 00:21:07.537
how to get over it and so in doing that,

00:21:07.537 --> 00:21:10.173
in my skating and also in the
business it was like, well,

00:21:10.173 --> 00:21:12.179
this is what's coming at us.

00:21:12.179 --> 00:21:13.691
We've got to figure out
how to navigate this

00:21:13.691 --> 00:21:15.287
and how to succeed and a
lot of people just give up

00:21:15.287 --> 00:21:18.166
and especially in skating, I mean,

00:21:18.166 --> 00:21:20.399
that's the first sign
of someone that's going

00:21:20.399 --> 00:21:22.943
to be successful is no matter how far,

00:21:22.943 --> 00:21:25.539
no matter how successful they
get especially in competition,

00:21:25.539 --> 00:21:27.554
they continue to challenge themselves

00:21:27.554 --> 00:21:29.378
because they want to improve.

00:21:29.378 --> 00:21:31.564
Those are the guys that
make it way further

00:21:31.564 --> 00:21:33.468
than anyone else.

00:21:33.468 --> 00:21:35.885
(punk music)

00:21:54.287 --> 00:21:55.913
(crowd cheering)

00:21:55.913 --> 00:21:56.915
- Your Honor,

00:21:56.915 --> 00:21:58.937
I'm here on behalf of the
great state of Alabama

00:21:58.937 --> 00:22:03.820
to prove once and for all
that skateboarding is, was,

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:07.720
and always has been a
crime and that this man,

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:10.887
Anthony Francis Haywood Beauford Hawk,

00:22:13.316 --> 00:22:16.636
should be found guilty of perpetrating

00:22:16.636 --> 00:22:18.969
the most gnarly of offenses.

00:22:19.966 --> 00:22:23.685
- Mr. Hawk, would you like
to make an opening remark?

00:22:23.685 --> 00:22:26.300
- Yeah, I guess that I think
skateboarding is not a crime.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:30.487
- There is no half pipe that
can launch you out of hell.

00:22:30.487 --> 00:22:33.237
(crowd clapping)

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:38.485
- And what was Mr. Hawk doin' at the time?

00:22:38.485 --> 00:22:40.585
- He was riding one of
those planks with wheels.

00:22:40.585 --> 00:22:41.585
- Objection.

00:22:41.585 --> 00:22:43.949
Skateboarding is allowed in public areas.

00:22:43.949 --> 00:22:45.930
- Well, I just thought that
doesn't seem like something

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:47.572
you should do.

00:22:47.572 --> 00:22:50.646
(crowd clapping)

00:22:50.646 --> 00:22:52.155
- Mr. Hawk, how long have you been riding

00:22:52.155 --> 00:22:53.822
these devil mobiles?

00:22:55.457 --> 00:22:56.463
- Pretty much my whole life.

00:22:56.463 --> 00:23:00.298
- Let the record show that Mr.
Hawk was carving a pentagram

00:23:00.298 --> 00:23:02.224
into his chest as he answered.

00:23:02.224 --> 00:23:03.231
- No, I wasn't!

00:23:03.231 --> 00:23:04.441
- Let me ask you something, boy.

00:23:04.441 --> 00:23:05.490
- I'm in my mid-40's.

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:07.611
- If God had intended us to skateboard,

00:23:07.611 --> 00:23:11.090
why wouldn't he have blessed
us with wheels instead of feet?

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:12.125
- I don't know.

00:23:12.125 --> 00:23:14.967
- Why did he give us
legs instead of trucks?

00:23:14.967 --> 00:23:17.920
Or why did he not put
little decals of skulls

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:20.615
on our bellies or put
adhesive tape on our back?

00:23:20.615 --> 00:23:21.615
- I get it.

00:23:21.615 --> 00:23:23.928
- As the Bible says, Lo,

00:23:23.928 --> 00:23:27.428
and man shall not step on a wheeled plank.

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:30.683
- Revelation 20:13.

00:23:30.683 --> 00:23:34.084
- Now, I personally can't
think of anything more sinful

00:23:34.084 --> 00:23:38.279
than somebody launching off
a vert ramp into a kick flip

00:23:38.279 --> 00:23:42.245
mctwist but for those who need convincin',

00:23:42.245 --> 00:23:44.911
maybe you could enlighten us, professor.

00:23:44.911 --> 00:23:47.117
- I've discovered several
eyewitness accounts

00:23:47.117 --> 00:23:51.832
of skateboarding at some of
history's greatest crimes.

00:23:51.832 --> 00:23:55.270
This is a picture of Tony Hawk
high fiving Adolph Hitler.

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:56.482
- That's obviously doctored.

00:23:56.482 --> 00:23:59.467
- This is Tony doing what
appears to be an ollie

00:23:59.467 --> 00:24:01.348
off the balcony moments
after Lincoln was shot.

00:24:01.348 --> 00:24:02.829
- This is insane!

00:24:02.829 --> 00:24:05.557
- And this is Tony grinding down the tail

00:24:05.557 --> 00:24:08.569
of a dinosaur moments
before the meteor hit

00:24:08.569 --> 00:24:10.988
that exterminated all dinosaur
life on the planet earth.

00:24:10.988 --> 00:24:13.195
- Well, that one's kind of true.

00:24:13.195 --> 00:24:15.569
- Well, I invented the railing to provide

00:24:15.569 --> 00:24:18.814
a stable grabbing surface for God fearing,

00:24:18.814 --> 00:24:20.667
stair climbing men and women,

00:24:20.667 --> 00:24:23.865
not to be grinded on by
some immorale beast trying

00:24:23.865 --> 00:24:27.391
to pull off a frontside board slide.

00:24:27.391 --> 00:24:30.172
Nothing human uses a railing like that.

00:24:30.172 --> 00:24:32.041
You have disgraced my work, sir!

00:24:32.041 --> 00:24:36.208
You have disgraced the
work of Phineas Q. Railing.

00:24:37.502 --> 00:24:38.919
- I rest my case.

00:24:41.439 --> 00:24:44.053
- I find you guilty, Mr. Hawk,

00:24:44.053 --> 00:24:47.068
and declare skateboarding a crime!

00:24:47.068 --> 00:24:48.068
- You can't!

00:24:48.905 --> 00:24:51.359
- Would you like to say
anything before I pass sentence?

00:24:51.359 --> 00:24:53.341
- There's nothing else
you could do to me, sir.

00:24:53.341 --> 00:24:55.128
- I hereby sentence you to change

00:24:55.128 --> 00:24:58.845
to a respectable mode of transportation.

00:24:58.845 --> 00:24:59.945
- What's that?

00:24:59.945 --> 00:25:02.565
- Bring in the scooter.

00:25:02.565 --> 00:25:03.398
- Lame!

00:25:05.412 --> 00:25:09.626
Alright, today is June 27th,
2016 and it is 17 years

00:25:09.626 --> 00:25:13.188
to the day that I landed
my first 900 at the X Games

00:25:13.188 --> 00:25:14.857
in San Francisco.

00:25:14.857 --> 00:25:16.865
A lot has happened since then in my life.

00:25:16.865 --> 00:25:18.959
I mean, it's been the
craziest roller coaster ride

00:25:18.959 --> 00:25:21.267
and it really was the apex
of my competitive career.

00:25:21.267 --> 00:25:23.600
So I'm going to try a 900 today

00:25:25.179 --> 00:25:29.388
because I feel like I can and
I never thought I'd be doing

00:25:29.388 --> 00:25:31.442
this at my age when I was young.

00:25:31.442 --> 00:25:32.879
I really didn't think
that was a possibility

00:25:32.879 --> 00:25:35.506
but I'm still goin' and
keep goin' 'til the wheels

00:25:35.506 --> 00:25:36.923
fall off I guess.

00:25:45.475 --> 00:25:47.475
(bleep)

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:23.543
(group cheering and clapping)

00:26:35.807 --> 00:26:37.210
Spencer was there on my first one

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:39.251
and now he was on my last.

00:26:39.251 --> 00:26:40.084
Bye.

00:26:42.803 --> 00:26:44.499
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:26:44.499 --> 00:26:46.944
I made this video because
VenomLeon asked me to.

00:26:46.944 --> 00:26:49.287
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:26:49.287 --> 00:26:50.388
to profile next,

00:26:50.388 --> 00:26:51.730
leave it down in the comments below

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:52.735
and I'll see what I can do.

00:26:52.735 --> 00:26:54.284
I also love to know what did Tony say

00:26:54.284 --> 00:26:55.716
that had the biggest impact on you?

00:26:55.716 --> 00:26:57.680
What lesson did you
learn that you're going

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:00.395
to immediately apply to your
life or to your business?

00:27:00.395 --> 00:27:02.256
Leave it in the comments
and I'm going to join

00:27:02.256 --> 00:27:03.552
in the discussion.

00:27:03.552 --> 00:27:05.066
Finally, I wanted to give a
quick shout out to Hugo Beyer.

00:27:05.066 --> 00:27:07.885
Hugo, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:27:07.885 --> 00:27:10.000
Your One Word, and taking
that awesome picture

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:12.098
and posting it on Twitter.

00:27:12.098 --> 00:27:13.498
I'm really curious to understand
what you were looking at

00:27:13.498 --> 00:27:14.331
in that picture.

00:27:14.331 --> 00:27:16.360
I love it, bro, and I really
appreciate the support.

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:17.903
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:27:17.903 --> 00:27:19.591
I believe in you.

00:27:19.591 --> 00:27:21.207
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:27:21.207 --> 00:27:23.390
and whatever your one word is.

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:24.223
Much love.

00:27:24.223 --> 00:27:25.723
I'll see you soon.

00:27:31.656 --> 00:27:34.204
- The moment when I knew I wanted to skate

00:27:34.204 --> 00:27:36.871
and not do other sports
was the first time I went

00:27:36.871 --> 00:27:41.283
to a skate park and saw
people flying out of pools.

00:27:41.283 --> 00:27:43.909
All my life, I was trying to find a buzz

00:27:43.909 --> 00:27:46.295
and I didn't really get
it from team sports.

00:27:46.295 --> 00:27:48.841
I was okay at baseball and
basketball but I didn't,

00:27:48.841 --> 00:27:52.067
I wasn't improving at a rate I could see

00:27:52.067 --> 00:27:54.427
and then when I went to the
skate park for the first time,

00:27:54.427 --> 00:27:58.729
I literally saw these guys
going into a pool and flying.

00:27:58.729 --> 00:28:00.413
I was like, that's it!

00:28:00.413 --> 00:28:04.090
And then I started trying to
figure out how to do that.

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:05.426
I did it in the little pools.

00:28:05.426 --> 00:28:09.701
I saw a picture of Steve
Caballero who was about my size

00:28:09.701 --> 00:28:12.150
but just a little bit
older and he was doing air

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:14.437
in Winchester like three or four feet out.

00:28:14.437 --> 00:28:17.254
I saw these guys, they were
older and more experienced,

00:28:17.254 --> 00:28:19.488
doing these things and
it looked intimidating

00:28:19.488 --> 00:28:20.521
but it looked awesome.

00:28:20.521 --> 00:28:24.402
When I saw Steve Caballero,
he was my size, near my age,

00:28:24.402 --> 00:28:26.643
and he was literally
flying and I was like,

00:28:26.643 --> 00:28:29.444
if he can do it, I can do that.

00:28:29.444 --> 00:28:32.179
I may not do it as well as
him or with as much style

00:28:32.179 --> 00:28:36.958
but I'm going to learn how to
do that and that was the moment.

00:28:36.958 --> 00:28:39.535
That was the moment when I
decided I was going to do that

00:28:39.535 --> 00:28:42.198
because I did like that
it was made me different

00:28:42.198 --> 00:28:45.374
and it was something that
I identify myself with

00:28:45.374 --> 00:28:48.176
and it's made me stand
apart from everyone else

00:28:48.176 --> 00:28:49.366
I went to school with,

00:28:49.366 --> 00:28:50.969
most other kids my age.

00:28:50.969 --> 00:28:53.974
I did appreciate that
but at the same time,

00:28:53.974 --> 00:28:56.216
I always wondered, well,
why don't they like this?

00:28:56.216 --> 00:28:57.549
This is amazing.

00:28:59.152 --> 00:29:01.557
It's super difficult.

00:29:01.557 --> 00:29:02.862
It's adrenaline filled.

00:29:02.862 --> 00:29:04.302
It's dangerous.

00:29:04.302 --> 00:29:06.348
There's a sense of accomplishment.

00:29:06.348 --> 00:29:08.345
You know, what are they
missing about this?

00:29:08.345 --> 00:29:11.816
And it was always, it just
never really hit in those days,

00:29:11.816 --> 00:29:15.669
and so yeah, I guess I would
be the one to either blame

00:29:15.669 --> 00:29:19.836
or credit for doing mainstream
stuff and, hopefully,

00:29:20.710 --> 00:29:23.891
making it acceptable
or making it authentic.

00:29:23.891 --> 00:29:25.028
- You ever scared out there?

00:29:25.028 --> 00:29:26.953
I mean, some of this stuff is just insane.

00:29:26.953 --> 00:29:29.902
Are you ever kind of like
at the top of something

00:29:29.902 --> 00:29:31.433
or about to do something and you go, geez,

00:29:31.433 --> 00:29:33.250
I could break my neck here.

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:36.583
- Yeah, for sure, but that is, you know,

00:29:38.180 --> 00:29:40.862
that's the best gauge of
should you be doing it or not?

00:29:40.862 --> 00:29:42.020
It's more about,

00:29:42.020 --> 00:29:44.196
for me it's about approaching
things with confidence.

00:29:44.196 --> 00:29:46.176
If I'm going to set out to do something,

00:29:46.176 --> 00:29:47.576
I've already convinced
myself that I can do it,

00:29:47.576 --> 00:29:48.777
that it is possible.

00:29:48.777 --> 00:29:50.374
If I go at it and I think,

00:29:50.374 --> 00:29:51.838
I don't know if this is going to work,

00:29:51.838 --> 00:29:52.845
that's when I get hurt.

00:29:52.845 --> 00:29:53.844
- [Interviewer] That's when you get hurt.

00:29:53.844 --> 00:29:54.849
- You know what I mean?

00:29:54.849 --> 00:29:56.388
You can't just throw
caution to the wind and go,

00:29:56.388 --> 00:29:57.454
well, let's see what happens
and especially at my age

00:29:57.454 --> 00:30:00.037
and having this much experience

00:30:01.295 --> 00:30:03.393
and having so many injuries along the way,

00:30:03.393 --> 00:30:06.819
I'm way more methodical
about learning things.

00:30:06.819 --> 00:30:10.891
It takes me way more
tries and I have to know

00:30:10.891 --> 00:30:14.472
that it's going to work before
I go out and try to make it.

00:30:14.472 --> 00:30:15.854
- [Interviewer] As a
new dad who loves sports

00:30:15.854 --> 00:30:17.644
and wants one day to inspire my new son,

00:30:17.644 --> 00:30:20.130
I am wondering what advice
you have that either

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:22.899
your Dad, Frank, gave you or
that you've given your kids.

00:30:22.899 --> 00:30:25.232
- To do what you love doing.

00:30:26.609 --> 00:30:31.210
Don't succumb to peer
pressure or adult pressure.

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:34.877
Find your voice, find
what you really enjoy,

00:30:35.774 --> 00:30:38.077
and even if it's something
different and it's something

00:30:38.077 --> 00:30:39.402
that's not considered cool,

00:30:39.402 --> 00:30:40.877
if you love doing it,
you will thrive in it,

00:30:40.877 --> 00:30:44.196
and eventually you'll be happy

00:30:44.196 --> 00:30:46.262
because you continue to do it.

00:30:46.262 --> 00:30:47.459
- [Interviewer] Is that what Frank --

00:30:47.459 --> 00:30:49.507
- My Dad, you know, I
was the youngest of four,

00:30:49.507 --> 00:30:52.980
and by the time I came
around he had seen it all

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:55.291
and so he's just like, go, just do it.

00:30:55.291 --> 00:30:57.896
Whatever it is and I chose to do the most

00:30:57.896 --> 00:31:01.633
sort of off beat thing of my siblings

00:31:01.633 --> 00:31:04.498
but he was really supportive
in it and I was lucky in that

00:31:04.498 --> 00:31:09.456
and that's the same kind of
approach I had to my kids.

00:31:09.456 --> 00:31:10.976
If they love doing it,

00:31:10.976 --> 00:31:12.765
I don't care if it's not
considered the cool thing

00:31:12.765 --> 00:31:15.232
or if it doesn't mean that they're

00:31:15.232 --> 00:31:16.785
going to get a varsity letter.

00:31:16.785 --> 00:31:18.908
I want them to be happy and I
want them to enjoy themselves.

